{"context":"Steward mixes deterministic orchestration with LLM workers. A naive 'no LLM output may drive control flow' rule is self-contradicted by the plan's own WIs: `sto:review-roles` gates completion on a review verdict, and `sto:discuss-phase` derives the next phase from recorded discussion — both key control flow on worker output. The moment a worker's free-form verdict gates a step, runs stop being reproducible.","decision":"Control flow reads ONLY typed, schema-validated CKOs — never free-form LLM text. A worker verdict (approve/reject, plan-good, etc.) must be captured as a typed artifact (schema + declared fields) that code reads deterministically; a code policy or a HUMAN owns the GATE, never the same LLM. CODE decides which/when/if (scheduling, selection, gating, retries, sizing); the LLM decides what (content) only inside ACP worker sessions. No untyped LLM control-flow.","consequences":"Runs are reproducible given identical inputs; gates are auditable. The typed-verdict + human-gate pair is the explicit, named escape hatch. Cost: every content-inflected decision needs a typed artifact and a policy; content quality depends on the worker, not the loop.","alternativesConsidered":"No LLM input to control flow, including typed (rejected: unachievable, will be ignored). LLM-decided scheduling (rejected: non-deterministic, unauditable). LLM may veto a step via free text (rejected: control-flow leakage)."}